course: dictionary
lesson: 4418
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Many words have more than one meaning. The context, meaning the words around the word, tells you which meaning the writer means. In «The kitchen had hams, herbs, onions and eggs hanging from the rafters», hams are food, because herbs, onions and eggs are food too.
explain: Ham can also be a verb. «Don't ham it up» means don't act in a silly, too-dramatic way. In a drama class, the words are about acting, so ham must be about how you act. Ask yourself: what is the rest of the sentence about?
explain: Check your meaning by putting it into the sentence. «Mum made a ham and cheese sandwich for my lunch» makes sense with food. Read the sentence again with the meaning you chose, and if it still makes sense, you are probably right.
fact: Ham has more than one meaning, so context tells you which one the writer means.
fact: «Ham» means meat from the back leg of a pig, usually salted or smoked.
fact: «Ham it up» means to act or show off in an exaggerated way.
fact: «Ham radio» is radio sent and received by amateurs as a hobby.
fact: «Hamburger» comes from Hamburg, a city in Germany, not from ham; people split the word into "ham" and "burger".
exercise: «The kitchen had hams, herbs, onions and eggs hanging from the rafters.» What is a ham here? | *Meat from the back leg of a pig | A hobby with radio | A silly actor who over-acts | A long wooden bench
exercise: In drama class the teacher says, «Don't ham it up.» What does she mean? | *Act naturally, without being too dramatic | Act in a very silly, too-big way | Cook a big pot of soup | Sing very loudly
exercise: Which sentence uses ham to mean food? | I like to ham it up on stage | Hamburgers are my favourite toast | *I put a slice of ham in my sandwich | Ham radio is my favourite hobby
test: In «He likes to ham it up whenever he is on stage», what does ham it up mean? | To eat a big meal | *To act in an exaggerated, showy way | To sing in a choir | To cook meat slowly
test: Where does the word «hamburger» come from? | From the word ham | From a farm in Scotland | *From Hamburg, a city in Germany | From a French word for bread
exam: Tom reads, «Stop hamming it up and just act normally, Sam!» What does hamming it up mean? | To hang meat up to dry | To play a radio | *To act in an exaggerated, showy way | To make a sandwich
